We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
  \min \left\{\int_{\Omega}f\left(x, Dv(x)\right)dx\,:\, v\in
  \mathcal{K}_\psi(\Omega)\right\}.
  \end{equation*} Given the growth restrictions imposed on the function $f$, with respect to the gradient variable, we find that when the obstacle function is bounded and satisfies certain regularity conditions, the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ being part of the Sobolev space, leads to a corresponding increase in the differentiability of the solution, provided the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$ belongs to a $W^{1, p+2}$. Notably, we address subquadratic growth conditions on the function $f$, i.e., where $f(x,\xi)\approx a(x)|\xi|^p$ and $1<p<2$ and map $a$ also belongs to a Sobolev space, where $\Omega$ and $x,\xi\in \Omega$. Furthermore, we establish the existence and differentiability of solutions for the associated regularized problems, utilizing the density of smooth functions in the space defined by the combination of Sobolev and Hölder norms, thereby leveraging the regularity of the partial map $x\mapsto D_\xi f(x,\xi)$. The methodology employed is an expansion of the results obtained for the autonomous case. The key aspects of the paper are built upon the works of Cagnetti, Klann, and Rapetti, Cagnetti and Klann, and the autonomous version. The former refers to the application of higher differentiability results in non-autonomous optimization problems involving convex functions, where the latter extends the Frammartino's result concerning the differentiability of the mapping that relates the obstacle function to the gradient of the solution, within the context of convex optimization. The approach taken utilizes the combination of the Sobolev space and the Hölder space, where the latter is achieved through the application of an extension theorem. Moreover, the paper includes an enhancement of the Frammartino's result, where the original growth condition $\|D_\xi f(x,\xi)\|_{\mathcal{L}(\xi^\perp)}\leq \Lambda(1+|\xi|^{
